        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"colette-le-ble-en-herbe-fc68e748f41f0172\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Within the narrator’s imagined future scene, “Je t’aime” primarily avows Vinca’s love for Phil. Its placement among words of surrender gives it slight commitment-like force, but the explicit undertaking is chiefly carried by the adjacent “fais de moi ce que tu voudras,” not by the love avowal itself. Nothing indicates that the words function as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Because the utterance occurs inside a fantasy of Vinca’s future capitulation, it has some relational-undertaking coloration; however, this does not warrant strong P support for the core phrase.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"imagined\",\"description\":\"A narrator-projected and explicitly uncertain future utterance by Vinca (“perhaps next year”), quoted as part of an imagined scene rather than actually spoken in the narrated present.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The narrator imagines that Vinca may next year fall at Phil’s feet and speak “a woman’s words,” including “I love you,” pleas for attention, and permission for him to do as he wishes. This frames the avowal as part of anticipated emotional and relational surrender. The contrast with her present childish dignity and resistance also suggests that the imagined words represent a future transition into adult-coded submission. Phil’s dislike of her resistance and desire that she be exclusively promised and available add an asymmetrical, possessive context, but they do not alter the core phrase’s predominantly truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“C’est peut-être l’an prochain qu’elle tombera à ses pieds et qu’elle lui dira des paroles de femme”\",\"supports\":\"The event is an uncertain, narrator-imagined future utterance and is framed as a prospective change in Vinca’s relational stance.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Phil! ne sois pas méchant... Je t’aime, Phil, fais de moi ce que tu voudras... Parle-moi, Phil...”\",\"supports\":\"“Je t’aime” avows a loving state; the surrounding pleas and offer of submission supply limited performative coloration while carrying most of the undertaking force themselves.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Mais cette année elle garde encore la dignité revêche des enfants, elle résiste”\",\"supports\":\"The imagined avowal is contrasted with Vinca’s current resistance, reinforcing its role in a projected scene of capitulation rather than expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Phil wants Vinca “confiante, promise à lui seul, et disponible.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding relational dynamic is possessive and asymmetrical, explaining the slight commitment-related pressure without making that the principal force of “Je t’aime.”\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the imagined avowal, while a low P score records its participation in a larger scene of relational surrender. Delivery intensity or reflex-like production is not represented, so E is unwarranted; no important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification concerns the imagined future “Je t’aime,” not an actual declaration in the narrated present.\"}"
        }
      ],
